Celluloid Graft

Creative Catalyst: Its originators were film projectionists scoring silent movies that never existed.

This sound is the audio equivalent of a flickering 16mm film reel caught in a storm. It subverts the Chicago Blues’ mournful linearity by injecting the jagged, off-beat "skank" of ska, but played at the languid, sweat-drenched tempo of a samba school at sunrise. It is claustrophobic yet spacious, characterized by "Optical-Grit"—a production technique where magnetic tape is physically etched with tiny, jagged grooves to mimic the crackle of a dusty projector gate, causing the harmonics to shift in pitch like a slipping film strip.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 78–88 BPM. A heavy, syncopated pulse that mimics the mechanical thrum of a projector motor rather than a human heartbeat.
*   Harmonic Palette: Blues-inflected Dorian and Phrygian modes, often clashing with bright, discordant brass stabs that resolve into unresolved minor-major seventh chords.
*   Production Signatures: 1. Optical-Grit: Scratching physical tape to induce erratic wow-and-flutter; 2. Celluloid Gating: Using the rhythm of a film frame-rate (24fps) to trigger aggressive sidechain compression on the horn sections; 3. Projection-Booth Reverb: A convolution reverb captured from a hollowed-out metallic ventilation duct in an abandoned cinema.
*   Dynamics: A stifling, constant intensity that suddenly "snaps" (as if a reel broke), dropping into total silence before erupting back into a frenetic, brass-heavy crescendo.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement began in the basement of the defunct Grand Palace Cinema in 1950s Chicago. Projectionists, bored by the absence of films, began rigging their equipment to broadcast raw audio signals while manually manipulating the light shutters. They wore tuxedo jackets stained with machine oil and celluloid slivers, a uniform that became the signature "Projectionist Noir" style. Their rituals involved playing at midnight in complete darkness, save for the rhythmic, pulsing amber light of the projector bulb, which the audience—a secret society of insomniacs and film historians—used to track the beat.

Central Instruments: A battered 1940s hollow-body electric guitar, a detuned trombone, a custom-built hand-cranked samba drum (the surdo), and a repurposed carbon-arc lamp hum.

For Fans Of: Tom Waits, The Skatalites (on a bad trip), Ibrahim Ferrer, and Portishead.

