Track 2: Cuidado With The Aperture
- Story & Context: Old Mags, the chief projectionist, stands before the audience confessing that she has been splicing her own memories into the rotation for twenty years. She touches the vibrating lens, feeling the heat of the bulb through her gloves, while the room begins to smell of burnt sugar and old floor wax. She whispers to the crowd, "Why do you sit there staring at ghosts when the truth is peeling off the reel?" 
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A woman in a grease-stained formal dress standing in front of a glowing, spinning lens, silhouettes of film frames projected onto her skin, hauntingly elegant.

Track 3: Greenhouse Grafting
- Story & Context: Following the Great Shutter Failure, the cinema was reclaimed by the city, only for the locals to find the screen covered in bioluminescent moss that pulsed in time with the missing soundtrack. The legend claims that if you stand in the center of the aisle during a lightning storm, the music of the projectors returns, grafting the memories of the vanished audience onto the vines climbing the walls. The sound of the brass is said to be the only thing that keeps the greenhouse from collapsing into a pile of rust. "We are the bloom that grows from the ruin of the frame."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A lush, overgrown abandoned theater interior, projector light beaming through thick vines and glowing flowers, ethereal, melancholic, decaying grandeur.

